=== prefab_item_grav_tug_rig
metadata:``` yaml
type: item/upgrade/engineering
key: grav_tug_rig
display_text: Heavy Tug Rig
art: container_1a
mode: install
targets: ship
consoles: engineering
tier: 3
price: 600
desc: Reinforced grav emitters - the ship hauls as if it were four.
```
    # BOUGHT, NEVER FOUND - and `mode: install` is what says so. _item_spawn_pool skips
    # every install item, because a permanent fitting lying loose in a debris field is odd
    # for all of them, not just this one. Before that this scattered at about one drop in
    # fourteen in every map calling terrain_spawn_pickups: a PERMANENT 4x hauling
    # multiplier in a pool that is otherwise five-minute buffs and ore.
    #
    # The type deliberately STAYS `item/upgrade/...`. Retyping it is the obvious way to
    # dodge the scatter and it backfires: consoles/debug.py builds its "Test Items" skip
    # set from items_of_category("upgrade") and drops everything else as a floating
    # pickup, so a retype would have the debug console spawning the very thing this is
    # meant to stop - in the one place you would be standing while testing it.
    #
    # Tug Rig Mk I at the end of this file is the found, expiring, weaker half.
    #
    # NOT a modifier. The library asks a mission how much a ship counts for when it is
    # PULLING (grav_tether_set_pull_bonus_fn -> lm_tether_pull_bonus), which reads the
    # FITTED flag below, so the rig feeds the beam lag, the tow drag, the power share and
    # the strain readout at once and stays out of what the ship WEIGHS - a rig must not
    # make you harder to grav-lock or make your own wreck pay more salvage.
    #
    # THE FLAG IS NOT THE ITEM KEY, and that distinction is load-bearing. `market_buy`
    # credits the purchase as `set_inventory_value(ship, "grav_tug_rig", n)`, so a flag
    # sharing that name would be raised by CARRYING the crate: the rig would work before
    # anyone activated it, and selling the crate would rip it back out of a fitted hull.
    #
    # And `mode: install` does not consume, so the crate survives the fitting. Left in the
    # hold a station buys it back for half of what it cost while the rig stays bolted on,
    # which quietly made a 600 credit permanent upgrade cost 300. It is fitted now, not
    # cargo, so the crate is spent here.
    set_inventory_value(UPGRADE_AGENT_ID, "grav_tug_rig_fitted", 1)
    set_inventory_value(UPGRADE_AGENT_ID, "grav_tug_rig", 0)
    ->END

=== prefab_item_hacking_virus
metadata:``` yaml
type: item/trap/hack
key: hacking_virus
display_text: Hacking Virus
art: alien_5a
mode: consumable
targets: ship, cockpit
desc: A cyber weapon. Whoever trips it takes weapons and engine system damage.
pickup_store_if_no_trigger: false
```
    # A TRAP, using the same pickup_trigger path as a buff (item_collect). Place an instance
    # with a `pickup_trigger` naming its victims - e.g. set_inventory_value(v.id, "pickup_trigger",
    # "raider | kralien") - so only they trip it; everyone else leaves it on the map
    # (pickup_store_if_no_trigger false). The effect runs on the ship that tripped it.
    blob = to_blob(UPGRADE_AGENT_ID)
    if blob is not None:
        blob.set("system_damage", 8, sbs.SHPSYS.WEAPONS)
        blob.set("system_damage", 8, sbs.SHPSYS.ENGINES)
    ->END

=== prefab_item_escape_pod
metadata:``` yaml
type: item/quest/rescue
key: escape-pod
display_text: Escape Pod
art: escape-pod
mode: quest
targets: ship, cockpit
desc: A life-support capsule. Carry it home - dock it to deliver whoever is inside.
```
    # A QUEST item, not an upgrade: it is carried and delivered, never activated, so the
    # body is empty. `item/quest/...` keeps it out of the random scatter
    # (terrain_spawn_items samples upgrade/resource only) and off the Upgrades tab, and
    # omitting `price` keeps it out of station markets.
    #
    # The art matters: escape-pod carries interactionradius 603 in shipData, which is what
    # makes //collision/interactive fire. Without a registered art item_spawn falls back to
    # a mesh with NO interaction radius, and the pod becomes uncollectable - see PRM-1.
    ->END

=== prefab_item_tug_rig_mk1
metadata:``` yaml
type: item/upgrade/engineering
key: tug_rig_mk1
display_text: Tug Rig Mk I
art: container_small_6a
mode: consumable
duration: 600
targets: ship
consoles: engineering
tier: 2
desc: An early-pattern grav rig - hauls as if the ship were two and a half, for a time.
```
    # The found half of the tug rig pair. `item/upgrade/...` on purpose here: this one IS
    # meant to be scattered, at tier 2, the same weight as the other tier-2 consumables.
    # No `price`, so it never reaches a station market - the Heavy Tug Rig is the thing
    # you buy, this is the thing you find. Half the pull (2x, not 4x) and it burns out.
    #
    # THE EFFECT IS THE TIMER. Nothing in this library undoes a consumable whose effect is
    # not a modifier: upgrade_add takes no duration, Upgrade has no expiry and no
    # deactivate hook, and the only self-expiring path is modifier_add(duration=) swept by
    # the modifier handler - which a pull bonus cannot use, because it is not a blob key.
    # prefab_item_secret_codecase above is what that looks like when it goes unnoticed: it
    # sets a flag with `duration: 300` and nothing ever clears it. So rather than set a
    # flag and hope something chases it, the window itself is the state, and
    # lm_tether_pull_bonus reads it.
    #
    # An `await delay_sim(duration)` here would also work - an upgrade body is a real
    # suspendable server task - but nothing binds that task to the holder, so a destroyed
    # ship would leave it running, and a mission reload rebuilds the scheduler and the
    # flag would never be cleared at all. A timer has no task and dies with the agent.
    #
    # is_timer_finished answers True for a timer that was never set, so a ship that never
    # fitted one reads as expired: the guard fails closed.
    set_timer(UPGRADE_AGENT_ID, "tug_rig_mk1", seconds=duration)
    ->END
